From: Adam <i@noc.cat>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 05:40:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 81/81] net: stmmac: tc956x: load the GPIO driver before probing

The PHY resets of both ports are GPIOs of the chip's own GPIO block,
which gpio_tc956x serves from an auxiliary device the PCI driver creates
together with the two Ethernet ones. udev loads both modules on those
devices' events in whatever order they arrive, and the GPIO driver
probes asynchronously, so a port could reach its PHY reset while the
GPIO chip was still binding. That port deferred and was retried later,
after the other one had registered: on a Radxa Dragon Q8B, one boot in
a dozen named function 1 eth0 and function 0 eth1.

Declare gpio_tc956x as a soft pre-dependency. modprobe then loads it
first, and the module loader waits for its asynchronous probe before
returning, so both ports find the GPIO chip ready and register in
device order: function 0 is eth0, function 1 eth1, on every boot.

---
 drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/dwmac-tc956x.c | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/dwmac-tc956x.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/dwmac-tc956x.c
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/dwmac-tc956x.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/dwmac-tc956x.c
@@ -867,3 +867,11 @@ module_auxiliary_driver(tc956x_dwmac_driver);

 MODULE_DESCRIPTION("Toshiba TC956x PCIe Ethernet Network Driver");
 MODULE_LICENSE("GPL");
+
+/*
+ * The PHY resets are GPIOs of the chip's own GPIO block, served by
+ * gpio_tc956x. Loading it first keeps both ports from probing while it is
+ * still binding, which would defer one of them and hand out eth0 and eth1
+ * in whichever order the retries happen to finish.
+ */
+MODULE_SOFTDEP("pre: gpio_tc956x");
